#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[$mu\(nothing\)= 0$ 的定义并非 redundant]
<muemptyset-0-的定义并非-redundant>
Let $\(X\,cal(A)\)$ be a measurable space. Is the condition $mu\(nothing\)= 0$ in the definition of a measure on $\(X\,cal(A)\)$ redundant? In other words, if $mu : cal(A) arrow.r\[0\,oo\]$ is a function such that
$ mu (union.big_(i = 1)^oo A_i) = sum_(i = 1)^oo mu\(A_i\)\, $
for any disjoint subsets $A_i in cal(A)$, $i in bb(N)$, does it follow that $mu\(nothing\)= 0$? If not, what can you say?

#proof[
It does not follow. \ Counterexample: Consider $mu\(E\)= oo #h(0em) #h(0em) forall E in cal(A)$. \ This measure satisfies the countably disjoint additivity condition, since for every disjoint sequence of sets in $cal(A)$, $mu (union.big_(i = 1)^oo A_i) = sum_(i = 1)^oo mu\(A_i\)= oo$ has infinite measure.

]
#heading(level: 2, numbering: none)[measurable set seq 的 limit 也 measurable (且如果 seq tail $sigma$-finite $arrow.r.double.long$ limit commute )]
<measurable-set-seq-的-limit-也-measurable-且如果-seq-tail-sigma-finite-implies-limit-commute>
Let $\(X\,cal(A)\,mu\)$ be a measure space, and let $A_i in cal(A)\,i in bb(N)$. Assume that the sets $A_i$ converge to the set $A subset X$ in the sense that:
\- If $x in A$, then $x in A_i$ for all but finitely many $i$\;
\- If $x in.not A$, then $x in.not A_i$ for all but finitely many $i$.

#strong[\(a) Prove that $A$ is measurable, that is, $A in cal(A)$.]

#proof[
Deduing from the conditions:
If $x in A$, then $x in A_i$ for all but finitely many $i$\; $arrow.r.double.long$ $A subset liminf A_i$
If $x in.not A$, then $x in.not A_i$ for all but finitely many $i$. $arrow.r.double.long$ if $x in A_i$ for all but finitely many $i$ then $x in A$ $arrow.r.double.long$ $limsup A_i subset A$
Thus
$ limsup A_i subset A subset liminf A_i $

#strong[Claim1: For any sequence of sets $\(A_i\)_(i in bb(N))$, we have
$ liminf A_i subset limsup A_i $]
Proof of Claim 1: Follows trivially from the definition, since $x in.not A_i$ for all but finitely many $i$ $arrow.r.double.long$ $x in.not A_i$ for infinitely many $i$. \ \ Combining claim (1) with (2.1) we have
$ limsup A_i = A = liminf A_i $
#strong[Claim 2: For any sequence of sets $\(A_i\)_(i in bb(N))$ in a $sigma$-algebra, $liminf_i A i$ and $limsup_i A_i$ is also in the $sigma$-algebra.] \ Proof of Claim 2: This follows from the def and fact that union and intersection of a countable sequence sets in a $sigma$-algebra is also in this $sigma$-algebra. We have

